51
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:17.360
This discussion examines euro dollar liquidity and the case for gradual financial strain, not an imminent collapse.

52
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.800
The stakes are how funding stress can spread.

53
00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:32.360
And the reason we think it's important is because we first of all, we we've said for a long time that we thought that Europe was in trouble for many reasons.

54
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:45.000
Um, but we titled it slow shave because we don't think it's helpful to just kind of come out and make these grandiose predictions all the time about collapse or a crisis or you know, a great depression.

55
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:55.640
But the point we've made for a long time is that part of the reason our thesis, the whole dollar milkshake theory, and we think that the the US is kind of the cleanest dirty shirt.

56
00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.960
For this to make sense, we're gonna have to go into the dollar milkshake theory itself.

57
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:06.280
So basically, to understand the dollar milkshake theory, you have to understand what's going on in the global markets.

58
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.880
So it's an idea that we've talked about before the euro dollar market.

59
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:24.760
So euro dollars are dollars that are outside of the control of the um central banking system here in the US, but banks that are being regulated by themselves over in Europe, though, it is not a euro dollar market as anything outside of the US.

60
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:29.080
It is not specifically just European, uh, but that is certainly a big part of it.

61
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:33.320
Is okay, they're able to create dollar-denominated debt.

62
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.800
So they spin debt up out of nowhere.

63
00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.240
They spin it up overnight.

64
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:40.200
This is exactly what happened in 2008.

65
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.520
The euro dollar market is like the respiratory system of the global economy.

66
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:52.040
So overnight you create debt, you destroy debt, um, or just don't make debt, which is precisely what happened in 2008.

67
00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.800
So everybody started to get far more cautious about what was going on in the global economy.

68
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:04.040
And so even loaning people money overnight became something that was um considered high risk.

69
00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:13.880
So people started doing less of it, and that meant the companies that weren't very solvent and couldn't actually survive the overnight transfers, ended up going out of business.

70
00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.840
And so that's what happened to Lehman Brothers.

71
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:29.680
They were able to traditionally get access to capital overnight, just hey, loan us, you know, whatever, a few few billion dollars so that we can cover everything, and then we'll be back to business in the morning and everything works.

